`signInWithPopup` is blocked by mobile browsers (iOS Safari, Android WebViews, in-app browsers), causing Google sign-in to silently fail on mobile.

## Changes

- **`AuthService`**: Branches sign-in strategy by input device type:
  - Coarse-pointer/touch devices (`pointer: coarse`) → `signInWithRedirect`
  - Desktop → `signInWithPopup` (unchanged)
- **Redirect result handling**: `getRedirectResult()` called on app init (browser-only) to complete the redirect flow when returning from Google's sign-in page
- **Device detection**: Uses `window.matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)')` via `isCoarsePointer()` — correctly handles iPadOS 13+, Samsung DeX, hybrid touch devices, and spoofed UAs; returns `false` safely during SSR
- **SSR safety**: Both `getRedirectResult()` and `isCoarsePointer()` are guarded with `isPlatformBrowser(PLATFORM_ID)` to prevent crashes during server-side rendering
- **Error observability**: Caught errors are passed to the logger with full details and rethrown with `{ cause: error }` to preserve the error chain
- **Unit tests**: Added `auth.service.spec.ts` covering redirect/popup branching, SSR guards, redirect result handling, and error logging

```typescript
private isCoarsePointer(): boolean {
  if (!isPlatformBrowser(this.platformId)) return false;
  return window.matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches;
}

async signInWithGoogle(): Promise<void> {
  // ...
  if (this.isCoarsePointer()) {
    await signInWithRedirect(this.auth, this.googleProvider);
  } else {
    await signInWithPopup(this.auth, this.googleProvider);
  }
}
```

When execution branches to a null/bad address (e.g. CTR=0 → `bcctr`), the zero-filled memory decodes as `Opcode::Illegal`, which was raising a `0x0700` Program Exception. The OS handler at `0x0700` increments SRR0 by 4 and `rfi`s back — creating an infinite loop stepping through the bad address space 4 bytes at a time.

## Changes

- **`crates/ppcir/src/decode.rs`**: `Opcode::Illegal` now emits `ReturnStatic(pc+4)` and records the address in `unimplemented_ops`, matching `ppcjit`'s existing `ignore_unimplemented` skip behavior.

```rust
// Before
Opcode::Illegal => {
    b.push(IrInst::I32Const(pc as i32)); b.push(IrInst::StorePC);
    b.push(IrInst::RaiseException(0x0700));
    return true;
}

// After
Opcode::Illegal => {
    b.unimplemented_ops.push(format!("Illegal @ 0x{:08X}", pc));
    b.push(IrInst::ReturnStatic(pc + 4));
    return true;
}
```

Valid game code never contains truly illegal encodings — reaching `Opcode::Illegal` always indicates a bad branch target, not a game intentionally triggering a Program Exception. Skipping is the correct emulator behavior and is already what the native JIT does.

## Summary

Windows `fc.exe` always reports the size-difference message as:

```
FC: [longer_file] longer than [shorter_file]
```

The previous code used `"shorter than"` when file2 was the larger file:

```c
// Before
if (Block->StartA > Block->StartB)
    wprintf(L"FC: %ls longer than %ls\n", Context->Path1, Context->Path2);
else
    wprintf(L"FC: %ls shorter than %ls\n", Context->Path1, Context->Path2);
//                   ^^^^^^^^^^^
// When file2 is larger, this prints "file1 shorter than file2"
// instead of "file2 longer than file1"
```

## Change

Always identify the longer file and use `"longer than"`, regardless of which argument it is:

```c
if (Block->StartA > Block->StartB)
    wprintf(L"FC: %ls longer than %ls\n", Context->Path1, Context->Path2);
else
    wprintf(L"FC: %ls longer than %ls\n", Context->Path2, Context->Path1);
```

The `FC_DIFF_TYPE_SIZE` block is only emitted when file sizes differ, so the equal-size case never reaches this code.


